#
# EXAMPLE — not active. wwand never migrates your configuration on its own.
#
# Copy this to /etc/uci-defaults/ to have the migration run once, on the next
# boot, unattended:
#
#     cp /usr/share/wwand/examples/99-wwand-migrate /etc/uci-defaults/
#     chmod +x /etc/uci-defaults/99-wwand-migrate
#     reboot
#
# uci-defaults scripts run once at boot and are removed afterwards when they
# exit 0, so this converts the configuration exactly once and then disappears.
#
# What it does: converts stock `proto qmi` (uqmi), `proto mbim` (umbim) and
# `proto ncm` (comgt-ncm) interfaces IN PLACE into the network-native wwand
# model — a `config wwand_modem` section plus `proto wwand` + `option modem` on
# the interface, all in /etc/config/network. The interface name, APN, PIN,
# credentials and firewall membership are preserved.
#
# After this, wwand owns those interfaces and the stock dialer no longer does.
# DISABLE THE STOCK PACKAGE for the modems you migrate, or two dialers will
# race for the same control device:
#
#     /etc/init.d/... stop      # whatever drove the interface before
#
# To see what WOULD change without touching anything, run the same tool by
# hand — this is the recommended first step:
#
#     /usr/libexec/wwand/migrate            # dry run, prints the plan
#     /usr/libexec/wwand/migrate --apply    # convert
#
# The same conversion is available interactively in LuCI under
# Network -> Modems -> "Migratable interfaces".
#
# migrate is idempotent: an already-converted configuration is left unchanged,
# so re-running it is safe.

[ -x /usr/libexec/wwand/migrate ] || exit 0

/usr/libexec/wwand/migrate --apply >/dev/null 2>&1

exit 0
